WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:06.900
If you’ve ever felt swamped by the chaos of&nbsp;
it all and crave some time alone in nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.480
why not venture to Gruinard Island? This&nbsp;
remote lump of land off the northwest coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:19.920
of Scotland is utterly uninhabited and devoid&nbsp;
of irritating modern conveniences like wifi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.640
running water or reliable shelter. Encircled&nbsp;
by the frosty waters of the North Atlantic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.840
you could even satisfy your&nbsp;
penchant for cold water swimming.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.980
But along with your speedos you might want to pack&nbsp;
your hazmat suit. And instead of sun cream - it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.500
Scotland - it’s probably a good idea to top&nbsp;
up your vaccinations - specifically the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.240
that protects you from deadly bacterial&nbsp;
infections. Oh, and don’t be alarmed if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.820
locals refer to your new holiday spot as ‘the&nbsp;
Island of Death’ - according to the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.820 --> 00:01:00.120
Ministry of Defence, it’s *perfectly*&nbsp;
safe to visit. Well, it is now anyway...

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:06.720
Because, for a solid 50 years, beginning in&nbsp;
the early 1940s, this two-square-kilometre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.600
island in the arse end of nowhere was one of&nbsp;
the most perilous places on earth thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:19.440
one of the most bizarre covert operations of&nbsp;
the Second World War: Operation Vegetarian.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.420
Despite the name, it wasn’t an assault on&nbsp;
humanity’s herbivores. Quite the contrary - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.420
was a crazy campaign against carnivores:&nbsp;
specifically the German ones. For that reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.860
it was arguably one of the most misnamed&nbsp;
military campaigns of the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.840
right up there with Operation Toenails, the&nbsp;
invasion of New Georgia by Allied troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.840
Operations Red Bean and Frequent Wind, which&nbsp;
must have been unpleasant for everyone involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.900
and Operations Beaver Cage and Viking Snatch,&nbsp;
the less said about those, the better.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.540
Operation Vegetarian was a proposed&nbsp;
bio-warfare campaign during World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.340
War II that aimed to sabotage the&nbsp;
German food supply by contaminating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.640
cattle with anthrax - the horrific bacterial&nbsp;
infection, not the American thrash metal band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.120
(though I’m just cattle wouldn’t&nbsp;
like that very much either).

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.060
The plan was the brain fart, I mean,&nbsp;
brainchild, of British scientist Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.740
Fildes, a distinguished biology boffin&nbsp;
who, three years into World War II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.000
was tasked with concocting a biological&nbsp;
weapon that could turn the tide on Hitler.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.280
His answer was simple: drop millions of&nbsp;
anthrax-laced linseed cakes across Germany during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.500
the summer when the nation’s cattle would be out&nbsp;
grazing. If everything went to plan, the cattle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.680
would consume the cakes and become infected,&nbsp;
subsequently infecting the people who ate *them*.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.580
If it turned out the cattle&nbsp;
didn’t like the taste of anthrax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.660
the cakes would simply dissolve the next&nbsp;
time it rained, poisoning the grass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.940
which the cows would eventually eat anyway. Either&nbsp;
way, things would end badly for the bovines.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:34.020
The bacteria that causes anthrax, Bacillus&nbsp;
anthracis, can be inhaled, ingested or absorbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.180
through direct contact with the skin, but the&nbsp;
side effects are ultimately the same: sepsis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.280
multiple organ failure, and swelling of the fluid&nbsp;
and membranes around the brain and spinal cord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.760
resulting in extensive internal&nbsp;
bleeding, and, eventually, death.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.580
Cutaneous anthrax - the one you get through&nbsp;
your skin - is the most common form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.400
and inhalation anthrax is the deadliest,&nbsp;
but gastrointestinal anthrax - the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:05.160
the British planned to spread through Operation&nbsp;
Vegetarian - isn’t far behind. Left untreated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.840
over half of gastrointestinal anthrax&nbsp;
patients perish, and even if treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:16.440
with the right antibiotics, which weren’t around&nbsp;
in 1942 anyway, 40% of patients don’t make it.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.600
Anthrax is not man-made. Bacillus anthracis&nbsp;
is found naturally all over the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.600
mainly in soil. It mostly infects farmed and&nbsp;
wild animals, who occasionally transmit it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.520
to humans. Some scholars think it was the&nbsp;
real cause of the 5th plague in the Bible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:38.160
which describes a sickness that afflicts&nbsp;
livestock. Today, we still witness roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.980
2,000 cases annually. Operation Vegetarian&nbsp;
would have dwarfed those numbers.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:49.500
The plan was to contaminate Germany’s&nbsp;
beef cattle, possibly infecting humans who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.600
feasted on the meat, but at the very least&nbsp;
killing all the cows, creating a colossal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.780
food shortage that would force the Nazis to&nbsp;
reroute resources and attention away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:06.120
beating the crap out of half of Europe. But&nbsp;
this logic was flawed from the outset. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.980
Paul Fildes started his research, Hitler&nbsp;
and his forces were at the peak of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:16.560
European expansion, granting them access to&nbsp;
food supplies far beyond Germany’s borders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.520
Killing a lot of local cows would have been&nbsp;
horrible, but it wouldn’t have starved the nation.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.780
So, not a particularly pragmatic strategy, but&nbsp;
that was far from the worst of it. Operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:37.620
Vegetarian, despite its tree-hugging name, was&nbsp;
an ethical catastrophe. Had it been carried out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.520
it would have been impossible to control&nbsp;
the spread of anthrax, which would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:47.400
infected not only the targeted cattle, but also&nbsp;
other animals, humans and even the land itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:53.820
potentially killing millions and rendering&nbsp;
all of Germany uninhabitable for generations.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:07:00.120
In fairness, the British didn’t know all of this&nbsp;
at the time. To discover anthrax’s full lethality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.020
they would first need to do the&nbsp;
sciency bit and test the stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:08.280
and for that they would need&nbsp;
a remote, unoccupied area far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:12.000
away from people - especially people&nbsp;
who were likely to ask inconvenient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:18.300
questions. Which brings us back to Gruinard&nbsp;
Island, your next dream holiday destination.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.400
In 1942, scientists from Porton Down, the&nbsp;
government’s biochemical research lab in southern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:29.760
England, descended on Gruinard Island, taking&nbsp;
60 sheep along for company. In the first test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.560
a modified plane dropped an anthrax bomb&nbsp;
on the island, killing all 60 sheepies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.780
but also some animals on the mainland&nbsp;
nearby. Later tests involving anthrax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:44.700
cake bombs caused more unplanned spread of&nbsp;
the disease when a contaminated sheep carcass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.620
washed up on a shore across the bay, sparking&nbsp;
an outbreak of anthrax in local animals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:55.560
livestock. The government told farmers a&nbsp;
passing Greek ship was to blame and hushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.900
everything up through the tactical use of some&nbsp;
very generous bribes - I mean compensation.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:07.440
Despite the collateral damage, anthrax bombs were&nbsp;
clearly a very deadly success. Gruinard Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:13.200
was transformed into a forbidding hellscape,&nbsp;
threatening death upon any setting foot on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.300
shores. So, what did the British military&nbsp;
do to remedy this environmental disaster?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.400
Carry out a thorough decontamination&nbsp;
program, restoring the once pristine island?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.680
Nah. They put up a big ‘no&nbsp;
entry’ sign, and buggered off.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:36.180
Now, if you’re thinking, ‘Wow, those anthrax&nbsp;
experiments sound terrifying - thank God the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:41.760
Brits abandoned the project and made themselves a&nbsp;
cup of tea instead’, you would be only partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.980
correct - there was almost certainly tea involved,&nbsp;
but the devastating tests on Gruinard Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:52.680
didn’t slow Operation Vegetarian down one&nbsp;
bit. Quite the opposite. Inspired by their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.960
newfound ability to kill lots of living things&nbsp;
within a few days, the British military entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:04.020
full-scale production, and by 1943 they’d&nbsp;
stockpiled 5 million anthrax cakes ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:10.020
for distribution across Germany. As the summer&nbsp;
of 1944 approached, Operation Vegetarian was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:17.580
officially ready to bear fruit. Or vegetables.&nbsp;
Actually, cake… Whatever. It was ready.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:23.940
But when the time came to execute the anthrax&nbsp;
apocalypse, the need for decimating an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.640
country with a murderous pathogen had disappeared.&nbsp;
With the successful invasion of Normandy behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:33.660
them, the Allies were steadily moving&nbsp;
across Europe, with the Nazis on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:39.780
retreat. Operation Vegetarian was abandoned, and&nbsp;
the 5 million anthrax cakes were incinerated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:45.060
along with all the taxpayers’ money that had&nbsp;
gone into making them. I think I speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:51.180
everyone and their cows when I say, thank&nbsp;
God. Not only would it have been an act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:57.060
of tremendous cruelty that would forever have&nbsp;
shifted the lines of what’s permissible in war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:01.140
the Brits still didn’t know the full&nbsp;
potential of what they were dealing with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:07.980
Yes, they had proved anthrax could be a lethal&nbsp;
weapon, but they had no idea how long its effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:14.580
would last. That they discovered when they&nbsp;
went back to Gruinard Island after the war.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:20.520
Scientists assumed the anthrax contamination&nbsp;
would neutralise itself after a few years, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:27.720
the spores proved to be incredibly resilient, and&nbsp;
decontamination efforts failed miserably. Gruinard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:33.060
remained a death trap for decades, a fact that&nbsp;
became public knowledge when a Ministry of Defence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:38.400
video was declassified in 1980. The footage&nbsp;
showed the experiments that had been performed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:58.500
almost 40 years before and resulted in a public&nbsp;
outcry. Just kidding, no one really gave a shit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.020
Well, *almost* no-one. A small band&nbsp;
of revolutionaries calling themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:11.700
rather ominously, the Dark Harvest Commando of the&nbsp;
Scottish Citizen Army… they gave lots of shits.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:18.060
These rebels first announced themselves to the&nbsp;
world in 1981 when a mysterious note was pinned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:23.220
the front door of St. Andrew’s House, home of the&nbsp;
Scottish Government in Edinburgh. They claimed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:29.460
have assembled a crack team of hard bastards&nbsp;
that included at least two microbiologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:36.060
and that they’d visited Gruinard island to collect&nbsp;
over 100 kilograms of poisoned soil. This soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.300
they threatened, would be left&nbsp;
in strategic locations around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:43.740
the country until the government&nbsp;
paid attention. Shortly afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.520
a package of anthrax soil was discovered&nbsp;
at Porton Down, the facility where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:53.400
Operation Vegetarian was developed. The Dark&nbsp;
Harvest Commandos appeared to mean business.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:58.080
Clearly unsettled, the British&nbsp;
government sprang into action…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:05.580
5 years later. In 1986 the worst of the&nbsp;
topsoil was removed, and everything left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:11.040
behind was sprayed with a mixture of sea&nbsp;
water and formaldehyde. In April 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:18.420
the quarantine was lifted, and Gruinard Island&nbsp;
was officially declared safe-ish to visit.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:24.000
In 1972 the Biological Weapons Convention was&nbsp;
created, banning countries from developing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:30.900
producing, stockpiling or using biological&nbsp;
weapons. 185 countries signed up, effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:35.640
making it illegal for governments and militaries&nbsp;
to muck about with substances like anthrax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:39.600
Hopefully, thanks to monstrous&nbsp;
mistakes like Operation Vegetarian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.500
we’re becoming a little smarter about&nbsp;
the downsides of mass destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:49.800
and can look forward to a more peaceful&nbsp;
world for all of mankind…… and cows.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.840
Thanks for watching.

